course: dictionary
lesson: 5429
title: Lah: a note, a word, a feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Lah» can be a note in music, or a short word used in speech in Singapore and Malaysia. In a music lesson the notes are sung «doh, ray, me, fah, soh, lah, te, doh», so lah is the sixth note there.
explain: When you read «Come on lah!» there is no music. The word lah is added at the end of a sentence to make it sound friendly, firm or relaxed. It is slang, not standard English, so you would not use it in a school essay.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Notes like do, re, mi mean music. A chatty sentence from Singapore or Malaysia means the slang word. Then ask: does this meaning fit the sentence?
fact: Lah (also la) is the sixth note of the scale do, re, mi, fa, so, la, ti, do.
fact: In Singapore and Malaysia, lah is slang added at the end of a sentence.
fact: «Come on lah!» can sound friendly, firm or relaxed, depending on how it is said.
fact: Lah is not standard English, so it belongs in casual talk, not in formal writing.
fact: «La-di-dah» means posh in a showy way, and it is informal British English.
exercise: In a music lesson the notes are sung «doh, ray, me, fah, soh, lah, te, doh». Which place is lah? | Fifth | *Sixth | Third | Seventh
exercise: «Come on lah!» What does lah do in this sentence? | It means stop | It turns the sentence into a music note | *It makes the sentence sound friendly, firm or relaxed | It shows the speaker is a music teacher
exercise: «La-di-dah» is used in the UK. What does it mean? | A quiet whisper | A sad song | A type of rain | *Posh in a showy way
test: In Singapore, a friend says «Can or not?» What does it mean? | *Is it possible? | Yes, sure, I agree | Tell me a joke | Please sing a note
test: Why might a Singaporean add «lah» to the end of a sentence? | To show they are singing | *To sound friendly, firm or relaxed | To make the sentence longer for a test | To ask a question about music
exam: Why is lah not used in a school essay? | It means posh | It is a music note | *It is not standard English | It is too short to count
